Finance allocates scarce capital across time and uncertain outcomes.|Compare future benefits with their cost and risk, then choose funding and investment together.|Spend ₹100 now for ₹115 next year: at a 10% required return, value today is ₹104.55.|A positive profit does not automatically imply value creation.|At a 20% required return, is this investment attractive?|No: present value is below ₹100|Yes: any profit is enough|Yes: timing does not matter
Personal finance manages household goals; corporate finance funds firms; public finance funds governments; investment finance prices claims.|The same cash-flow logic operates under different objectives, constraints, and responsibilities.|A household saves for retirement, a firm builds a factory, and a government funds a bridge.|Government objectives include public benefits that market prices may miss.|Which evaluation must explicitly consider public benefits?|A government bridge proposal|Only a household deposit|Only a quoted share price
Households supply savings; firms and governments raise funds; intermediaries connect them.|Banks transform funding and screen borrowers; markets issue and trade securities.|A household deposit funds a bank that lends to a business, subject to capital and liquidity constraints.|Intermediation changes risk; it does not eliminate it.|Who normally bears the business loan's credit risk directly?|The lending bank|The factory's customers only|Nobody after intermediation
Real assets produce goods or services; financial assets are contractual or ownership claims.|A financial claim distributes the cash flows of underlying activities.|A factory is a real asset; its owner's shares are financial assets.|A share's trading gain does not itself build another factory.|Which item directly provides productive capacity?|A machine|A bond certificate|A brokerage account statement
Income and expenses measure activity; assets and liabilities measure resources and obligations; equity is the residual.|Net worth equals assets less liabilities; retained income may increase it.|Assets ₹12 lakh less debt ₹5 lakh gives net worth ₹7 lakh.|Income is not the same as wealth.|Borrow ₹1 lakh and hold the cash. Net worth initially…?|Stays unchanged|Rises ₹1 lakh|Falls ₹1 lakh
Stocks are measured at a date; flows over an interval.|Opening stock plus net flows and valuation changes gives closing stock.|Cash opens at ₹10 and rises ₹3 during a month, closing at ₹13.|Do not compare annual revenue directly with a closing balance without a consistent ratio.|Which is a flow?|Annual sales|Year-end debt|Closing inventory
Saving defers consumption; investing seeks future benefits; borrowing and lending exchange funds; speculation accepts risk; hedging offsets it.|A hedge is judged alongside the exposure it protects.|An importer buys dollars forward to stabilise its future rupee payment.|A hedge can lose money while protecting the combined position.|A forward loses but the import becomes cheaper. Assess…?|The combined import and hedge|Only the forward loss|Only the forward notional
Sources supply cash; uses absorb it.|Every funding plan needs a matching use and timing schedule.|A ₹100 machine is funded with ₹60 equity and ₹40 debt.|Balanced funding does not establish that the investment is worthwhile.|Funding totals ₹90 for a ₹100 purchase. The plan has…?|A ₹10 funding gap|₹10 profit|No funding problem
Liquidity is near-term payment capacity; profitability is earnings; solvency is longer-term financial viability; flexibility is room to respond.|Examine timing, leverage, cash buffers, and access to funding separately.|A profitable firm owes ₹20 tomorrow but has ₹5 available.|Accounting profit cannot settle a bill until usable cash is available.|What is the immediate issue?|Liquidity|Gross margin alone|Share count alone
Return compensates investors for time and risk; opportunity cost is the best foregone alternative.|Use a required return appropriate to the cash flow's risk.|A project earns 8% when a comparable-risk alternative offers 10%.|High expected return is not guaranteed realised return.|The project's economic attraction is…?|Weak relative to the alternative|Proven by a positive return|Independent of alternatives
Accounting profit deducts recognised expenses; economic profit also deducts the opportunity cost of capital.|Economic profit = NOPAT − invested capital × required return.|NOPAT ₹15 on capital ₹100 at 12% gives economic profit ₹3.|Accounting interest expense is not the full opportunity cost of equity.|If NOPAT falls to ₹10, economic profit is…?|−₹2|₹10|₹22
Value creation increases total economic surplus; value transfer redistributes existing surplus.|Compare combined value before and after the action, including costs.|A negotiation moves ₹5 from buyer to seller without changing the asset's cash flows.|A higher seller price need not create total value.|This negotiation primarily creates…?|A transfer between parties|₹5 of new total value|A new productive asset
Certainty specifies outcomes; risk permits meaningful probability estimates; uncertainty makes those estimates fragile.|Use expected values under risk and robust scenarios under deep uncertainty.|A ₹100 payoff occurs with 60% probability and zero otherwise: expected payoff is ₹60.|An expected value is not a promised payment.|Does the investor necessarily receive ₹60?|No: it is a probability-weighted average|Yes: it is a guarantee|Yes: probabilities smooth each outcome
Agency problems arise when decision-makers' incentives diverge; information asymmetry hides facts; moral hazard changes behaviour after protection.|Align rewards, monitoring, and accountability without encouraging excessive risk.|A manager paid only for revenue grants costly customer credit.|More disclosure alone may not correct distorted incentives.|Which reward better addresses this behaviour?|Cash collection and risk-adjusted profitability|Revenue alone|Number of invoices alone
Arbitrage exploits inconsistent prices for equivalent payoffs; one-price logic requires comparable risks and trading conditions.|Market efficiency concerns information in prices, not perfect foresight.|Identical deliverable claims cost ₹98 and ₹100; costs of ₹3 can remove the apparent arbitrage.|Trading, funding, and short-sale constraints matter.|Is the ₹2 difference a profitable arbitrage with ₹3 costs?|No|Always|Only because both prices are positive
